# Downstream: keep the client password out of everything the operator runs,
# so a changed auth Secret is applied live with no pod restart and no
# failover. See operator/redisfailover/service/password.go.
#
# Replication, the Sentinels, the probes and the shutdown hook authenticate
# as an internal ACL user that may run only the commands they send, reads no
# key and no configuration parameter, and uses no channel but the Sentinels'
# hello channel. The exporter authenticates as a user of its own, the only
# operator user that may run CONFIG GET. Their passwords are in their own
# Secret (rfr-<name>-internal): a pod password, read by the probes at start,
# and an exporter password, both never rotated and never on a server but as
# a hash; and a link password, which masterauth and the Sentinels'
# auth-pass carry. The auth Secret's password is the default user's alone.
# A change to it is one MULTI/EXEC per running server that resets the
# operator's users, deletes any other user, sets requirepass, and kills
# every connection of the default and exporter users, so no connection
# that authenticated with the old password, or with anything its holder
# set, can read the new one; every mint and revoke disconnects the tenant's
# clients. Holders of the old password can read masterauth, so the link
# password is then rotated in stages that keep the old one valid until no
# server, replica or Sentinel uses it, and ends by disconnecting every
# MONITOR and every connection of an operator user from an address that is
# no pod of the RedisFailover and not loopback, so a replication stream or
# MONITOR opened before the change does not outlive it; the ConfigMap
# records the new client password only once all of it is done. A RedisFailover from before this patch
# gets the users live on every server and Sentinel before its ConfigMap and
# pod template switch to them, which rolls the pods once. After that every
# pass re-asserts the users, masteruser/masterauth and the Sentinels'
# auth-user/auth-pass, since the tenant's default user can change them, and
# a guard checks the internal user on every server every 2 s from memory,
# so a tenant's ACL DELUSER is undone before the Sentinels fail the master
# over. Every rotation and every guard run also deletes any ACL user but
# default, rf-internal, rf-exporter and pinger: one the tenant created would
# outlive a revoke, and with no aclfile none survives a restart anyway.
#
# The pod roll also moves the master role off a stale master with SENTINEL
# FAILOVER and relabels the pods before rolling it as a replica, instead of
# deleting the master and waiting for the Sentinels to notice, and ends that
# pass right after, so nothing heals towards the master it read before. Two
# masters are settled on the one a quorum of Sentinels names.
#
# Requires Redis or Valkey 7 or newer (CONFIG SET of several parameters,
# ACL subcommand rules).
# It builds on tls.diff (tlsConfig arguments) and unauthenticated-sentinel.diff
# (SetSentinelAuthPass); the Dockerfile applies /patches/*.diff in glob order,
# which the zz- prefix keeps it last in.
#
